
The partial unique index is the key part. Two people tapping "Reserve" on the same table at the same second can't both succeed, because MongoDB rejects the second insert. Catch the duplicate-key error and return "Table just taken".

Use a hold pattern. When a user starts a reservation it becomes pending for about 10 minutes. A cleanup job, or a scheduled updateMany, moves expired holds to expired so the table frees up.

Estimate
Piece	Time
Tables setup per club (admin) and availability endpoint	3–4 days
Reservation flow: pick night, table and party size, hold, confirm	4–5 days
Status rules: cancel, expire, no-show, cancellation window	2–3 days
Club admin dashboard: tonight's reservations, accept or decline, check-in by QR	4–5 days
Notifications: confirmation and reminder, ideally SMS	2–3 days
Online deposit (optional)	+5–8 days
Testing, especially concurrency	3–4 days
Payment
If clubs take deposits at the door or by transfer, skip online payment in version 1 and mark paymentStatus by hand. That saves about a week.
If you want online deposits, look at Telebirr or a local gateway like Chapa. Check their current API access and merchant onboarding before committing. Onboarding can take longer than the coding, and you'll need refund handling for cancellations.
What I'd do
First version: tables, holds, confirm or decline by the club admin, QR check-in, and no online payment.
Add SMS reminders.
Add deposits only after clubs have used the basic flow.

A rule worth deciding early is the cancellation policy, for example free cancellation until 6pm on the night. It affects the status logic, the refunds and the no-show handling.

Is a reservation here a table booking, or something simpler like a guest list? That one answer decides the real scope.